OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026", "title": "A counterexample to Naimark's problem in ZFC", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/naimark-counterexample-zfc.pdf", "family": "297", "family_title": "A ZFC counterexample to Naimark's problem", "abstract_html": "We construct in ZFC a unital infinite-dimensional simple complex <i>C</i><sup>∗</sup>-algebra with a faithful tracial state whose nonzero irreducible representations are all unitarily equivalent. This gives a negative answer to Naimark's problem without additional set-theoretic assumptions.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Flagship result:", "statement": "<p>**Flagship result: paper Theorem 1.1.**</p><p>All \\(C^*\\)-algebras and Hilbert spaces are complex. A representation is irreducible if it is nonzero and has no nontrivial closed invariant subspace. A tracial state \\(\\tau\\) is faithful if \\(\\tau(a^*a)&gt;0\\) whenever \\(a\\ne0\\).</p><p>There exists, in ZFC, a unital infinite-dimensional simple \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(A\\) with a faithful tracial state such that all its nonzero irreducible representations are unitarily equivalent. In particular, \\(A\\) is not isomorphic to \\(K(H)\\), the algebra of compact operators on any complex Hilbert space \\(H\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>**External homogeneity input: paper Theorem 6.1, Kishimoto–Ozawa–Sakai.** If \\(B\\) is a separable \\(C^*\\)-algebra and pure states \\(h,k\\) of \\(B\\) have GNS representations with the same kernel, there is an automorphism \\(\\gamma\\) of \\(B\\) such that\n\\[\nh\\circ\\gamma=k.\n\\]\nIn particular, this holds for every pair of pure states of a separable simple \\(C^*\\)-algebra. This is [8, Theorem 1.1], which in fact provides an asymptotically inner automorphism. It will be applied only to the separable algebras \\(A[S]\\) of Proposition 7.</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\mathfrak c=2^{\\aleph_0},\\qquad \\kappa=\\mathfrak c^+.\n\\]\nEvery ordinal below \\(\\kappa\\) has cardinality at most \\(\\mathfrak c\\), and every countable subset of \\(\\kappa\\) is bounded: otherwise \\(\\kappa\\) would be a countable union of sets of cardinality at most \\(\\mathfrak c\\), a contradiction.</p><p>**Fix the schedule first.** Before constructing any algebras, choose an injection\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\kappa\\times\\kappa\\longrightarrow\\kappa,\n\\qquad \\sigma(\\beta,\\eta)\\ge\\beta. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nTo do so, enumerate \\(\\kappa\\times\\kappa\\) in order type \\(\\kappa\\). When a pair \\((\\beta,\\eta)\\) is encountered, choose an unused ordinal at least \\(\\beta\\). Fewer than \\(\\kappa\\) ordinals have been used, whereas the tail above \\(\\beta\\) has cardinality \\(\\kappa\\). This defines the entire schedule without referring to any states.</p><p>**Construct the algebras in ordinal order.** Build a continuous bridge iteration\n\\[\n(X_\\beta)_{\\beta\\le\\kappa}\n\\]\nstarting from\n\\[\nX_0=A_0=\\bigotimes_{m=1}^{\\infty}M_2,\n\\qquad\np_0=1,\\quad p_n=e_{11}^{\\otimes n}\\otimes1.\n\\]\nFor each \\(\\beta&lt;\\kappa\\), once \\(X_\\beta\\) has been constructed, choose a list\n\\[\n(h_{\\beta,\\eta})_{\\eta&lt;\\kappa}\n\\]\ncontaining every pure state of \\(X_\\beta\\), with repetitions allowed. This is possible by Proposition 9, since \\(|\\beta|\\le\\mathfrak c\\).</p><p>At stage \\(j&lt;\\kappa\\), let \\(f\\) be the state of \\(X_j\\) peaked by \\((p_n)\\). If \\(j\\) is outside the range of \\(\\sigma\\), make the identity step. Otherwise write\n\\[\nj=\\sigma(\\beta,\\eta)\n\\]\nand let \\(g\\) be the unique lift of \\(h_{\\beta,\\eta}\\) to \\(X_j\\). The list for \\(\\beta\\) is already available because \\(\\beta\\le j\\); when \\(\\beta=j\\), it is chosen before the successor step. If \\(g\\sim f\\), again make the identity step.</p><p>Suppose \\(g\\not\\sim f\\). By Proposition 9, choose a countable dependency-closed \\(S\\subset j\\) such that \\(g|_{A[S]}\\) is pure and uniquely determines \\(g\\) on \\(X_j\\). The restriction \\(f|_{A[S]}\\) is pure by peaking under restriction from Proposition 1. The algebra \\(A[S]\\) is separable, simple, and unital by Proposition 7. Apply the external homogeneity theorem to obtain an automorphism \\(\\gamma\\) of \\(A[S]\\) with\n\\[\n(g|_{A[S]})\\circ\\gamma=f|_{A[S]},\n\\qquad\nq_n^j=\\gamma(p_n)\\quad(n\\ge0).\n\\]\nIf a state on \\(A[S]\\) takes value one on every \\(q_n^j\\), composing it with \\(\\gamma\\) gives the unique state taking value one on every \\(p_n\\). Thus \\((q_n^j)\\) peaks to \\(g|_{A[S]}\\). By Proposition 8, it peaks to \\(g\\) on \\(X_j\\) as well.</p><p>For the trace requirement, \\(\\gamma\\) carries the unital matrix stage \\(M_{2^n}\\subset A_0\\) onto a unital matrix subalgebra of \\(A[S]\\), and carries its minimal projection \\(p_n\\) to \\(q_n^j\\). The ambient trace restricts to the unique normalized trace on this matrix algebra. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\tau(q_n^j)=2^{-n}=\\tau(p_n).\n\\]\nThis argument does not require an automorphism to preserve the specified trace on all of \\(A[S]\\).</p><p>Perform the bridge of Proposition 5 with \\((p_n),(q_n^j)\\), and set \\(I_j=S\\). At an identity step set \\(I_j=\\varnothing\\). At limit stages take the norm completion of the union. Each successor satisfies \\((U)\\) and has a faithful trace-preserving expectation onto its base. Propositions 6–9 therefore apply throughout the recursion.</p><p>All choices are set-sized. At every stage the algebra is generated by at most \\(\\kappa\\) elements, so its density is at most \\(\\kappa\\) and its underlying cardinality at most\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\kappa^{\\aleph_0}.\n\\]\nFix a set of cardinality \\(\\lambda\\). Algebra structures on its subsets, together with their norms, traces, embeddings, states, automorphisms, and lists, form a set of codes. Well-order that set to make the required choices; recode successor algebras with their embeddings and use norm inductive limits. Ordinary choice and transfinite recursion in ZFC suffice.</p><p>**Every final pure state is treated.** Let\n\\[\nA=X_\\kappa\n\\]\nand let \\(h\\) be any pure state of \\(A\\). By Proposition 9, there is a countable dependency-closed \\(S\\subset\\kappa\\) such that \\(h|_{A[S]}\\) is pure and has unique extension \\(h\\) to \\(A\\). Boundedness of \\(S\\) gives \\(\\beta&lt;\\kappa\\) with\n\\[\nA[S]\\subset X_\\beta.\n\\]\nThe state \\(h|_{X_\\beta}\\) is the unique extension of \\(h|_{A[S]}\\) to \\(X_\\beta\\): any other extension could itself be extended to \\(A\\), contradicting uniqueness there. Hence \\(h|_{X_\\beta}\\) is pure by Proposition 1.</p><p>Choose \\(\\eta\\) with\n\\[\nh_{\\beta,\\eta}=h|_{X_\\beta}\n\\]\nand put \\(j=\\sigma(\\beta,\\eta)\\). Its lift to \\(X_j\\) is \\(h|_{X_j}\\). At stage \\(j\\), this lift either already belongs to the \\(p_n\\)-class or is joined to it by the bridge. By Proposition 6, the equivalence persists to \\(A\\). Thus all pure states of \\(A\\) have unitarily equivalent GNS representations.</p><p>Every nonzero irreducible representation \\(\\pi\\) of the unital algebra \\(A\\) is unital: the range of \\(\\pi(1)\\) is a nonzero closed invariant subspace. Every nonzero vector is cyclic by irreducibility, so each unit vector defines a pure state whose GNS representation is equivalent to \\(\\pi\\). The \\(p_n\\)-state supplies one such representation. Therefore \\(A\\) has exactly one nonzero irreducible representation up to unitary equivalence.</p><p>Finally, \\(A\\) is simple and has a faithful tracial state by Propositions 6–7. It is unital and contains the infinite-dimensional algebra \\(A_0\\). An algebra \\(K(H)\\) is unital only when \\(H\\) is finite-dimensional: the identity on an infinite-dimensional Hilbert space is not compact, as an infinite orthonormal sequence has no norm-convergent subsequence. The finite-dimensional case, including \\(H=0\\), cannot be isomorphic to \\(A\\). This proves the theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Pure states, unique extensions, peaking, and excision:", "statement": "<p>**Pure states, unique extensions, peaking, and excision: paper Lemmas 2.1–2.7.**</p><p>All algebras in this proposition are unital, and inclusions preserve the unit. For a state \\(h\\) of an algebra \\(B\\), write \\((\\pi_h,H_h,\\xi_h)\\) for its GNS representation. For pure states, write \\(h\\sim k\\) when \\(\\pi_h\\) and \\(\\pi_k\\) are unitarily equivalent. Put\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ad}(u)(a)=uau^*.\n\\]\nThe support projection \\(r_h\\in B^{**}\\) is the least projection on which the normal extension of \\(h\\) takes value one. Bounded functionals are extended normally to \\(B^{**}\\), with the same notation.</p><p>For an inclusion \\(B\\subset C\\), property \\((U)\\) means:\n\\[\n\\text{every pure state of \\(B\\) has exactly one state extension to \\(C\\)}.\n\\]\nUniqueness is among all states.</p><p>A decreasing sequence of projections \\((P_n)_{n\\ge0}\\) in \\(B\\) peaks to a state \\(h\\) if \\(h\\) is the only state satisfying \\(h(P_n)=1\\) for every \\(n\\).</p><p>The following hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A unique state extension of a pure state is pure. Equivalent pure states of \\(B\\) are conjugate by a unitary of \\(B\\); if both have unique state extensions to \\(C\\supset B\\), their extensions are equivalent.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If a decreasing projection sequence in \\(B\\subset C\\) peaks to a state \\(H\\) of \\(C\\), it peaks to \\(H|_B\\) in \\(B\\), and \\(H\\) is the unique state extension of \\(H|_B\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Every pure state \\(h\\) of \\(B\\) admits a decreasing net of positive contractions \\(x_\\lambda\\in B\\) such that\n\\[\nh(x_\\lambda)=1,\\qquad x_\\lambda\\downarrow r_h\n\\]\nstrongly in \\(B^{**}\\), where \\(r_h\\) is minimal, and\n\\[\nr_har_h=h(a)r_h,\\qquad\n\\|x_\\lambda(a-h(a)1)x_\\lambda\\|\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad(a\\in B).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If decreasing projections \\((P_n)\\) peak to \\(h\\), then \\(h\\) is pure,\n\\[\nP_n\\downarrow r_h,\\qquad\n\\|P_n(a-h(a)1)P_n\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nIf \\(h(a)=0\\), then \\(\\|P_naP_t\\|\\to0\\) on the product index set.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. If \\(h\\not\\sim k\\) are pure states and \\((x_\\lambda)\\), \\((y_\\nu)\\) are their decreasing excision nets as above, then\n\\[\n\\|x_\\lambda ay_\\nu\\|\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad(a\\in B)\n\\]\non the product directed set. Either net may instead be a peaking sequence of projections.</p><p class=\"li num\">6. If \\(B\\) has no finite-dimensional irreducible representations and \\(\\tau\\) is a tracial state on \\(B\\), then \\(\\tau(r_h)=0\\) for every pure state \\(h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.3 prove the assertions about unique extensions, unitary conjugacy, and peaking under restriction. Lemmas 1.4–1.5 give decreasing excision and projection excision, including minimality of pure-state support projections. Lemma 1.6 gives cross-excision, and Lemma 1.7 gives trace-nullity of pure-state supports.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(B\\subset C\\) be a unital inclusion.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(B\\subset C\\) be a unital inclusion. If a pure state \\(h\\) of \\(B\\) has a unique state extension \\(\\widetilde h\\) to \\(C\\), then \\(\\widetilde h\\) is pure.</p>", "proof": "<p>In a nontrivial convex decomposition of \\(\\widetilde h\\), both restrictions to \\(B\\) equal \\(h\\) by purity. Uniqueness then makes both states equal to \\(\\widetilde h\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Equivalent pure states of a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\) are conjugate by a unitary of \\(B\\).", "statement": "<p>Equivalent pure states of a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\) are conjugate by a unitary of \\(B\\). Consequently, if they each have a unique state extension to an algebra \\(C\\supset B\\), their extensions are equivalent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Realize the two states by unit vectors \\(\\xi,\\eta\\) in a common irreducible representation \\(\\pi\\). On their finite-dimensional span choose a unitary carrying \\(\\xi\\) to \\(\\eta\\) and a self-adjoint logarithm of that unitary. Kadison transitivity, including its self-adjoint form [7, Theorem 1], supplies a self-adjoint \\(a\\in B\\) whose action on this span is the chosen logarithm. The span reduces \\(\\pi(a)\\), so \\(u=e^{ia}\\) satisfies \\(\\pi(u)\\xi=\\eta\\). If \\(h\\) is represented by \\(\\xi\\) and \\(k\\) by \\(\\eta\\), then\n\\[\nk=h\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(u^*).\n\\]\nConjugating their unique extensions by the same unitary proves the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "peaking under restriction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(B\\subset C\\) be a unital inclusion, and let \\((P_n)\\) be a decreasing sequence of projections in \\(B\\) that peaks to a state \\(H\\) of \\(C\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(B\\subset C\\) be a unital inclusion, and let \\((P_n)\\) be a decreasing sequence of projections in \\(B\\) that peaks to a state \\(H\\) of \\(C\\). Then \\((P_n)\\) peaks to \\(h=H|_B\\) in \\(B\\), and \\(H\\) is the unique state extension of \\(h\\) to \\(C\\).</p><p>A state specified uniquely by peaking constraints is pure.</p>", "proof": "<p>A state specified uniquely by peaking constraints is pure: both states in any convex decomposition satisfy the same constraints.</p><p>Every state of \\(B\\) satisfying the projection constraints extends to a state of \\(C\\) with the same constraints, hence to \\(H\\). It must therefore equal \\(h\\). Every extension of \\(h\\) also satisfies the constraints and must equal \\(H\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "decreasing excision", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(h\\) be a pure state of a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(h\\) be a pure state of a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\). There is a decreasing net of positive contractions \\(x_\\lambda\\in B\\) such that \\(h(x_\\lambda)=1\\), \\(x_\\lambda\\) decreases strongly to the minimal projection \\(r_h\\in B^{**}\\), and, for every \\(a\\in B\\),\n\\[\n\\|x_\\lambda(a-h(a)1)x_\\lambda\\|\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nr_har_h=h(a)r_h\\qquad(a\\in B).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nL=\\{a\\in B:h(a^*a)=0\\},\\qquad N=L\\cap L^*.\n\\]\nHere \\(L\\) is a closed left ideal and \\(N\\) is a \\(C^*\\)-subalgebra. If \\(a\\in L\\), then \\(a^*a\\in N\\), since\n\\[\nh((a^*a)^2)\\le \\|a\\|^2h(a^*a)=0.\n\\]</p><p>First, \\(h\\) is the only state annihilating \\(N\\). If another state \\(k\\) annihilates \\(N\\), then \\(L\\) is contained in the kernel of \\(a\\mapsto\\pi_k(a)\\xi_k\\). The map\n\\[\nQ_h:B\\longrightarrow H_h,\\qquad Q_h(a)=\\pi_h(a)\\xi_h,\n\\]\nis onto by one-vector Kadison transitivity. This assertion concerns the whole Hilbert space, even when it is nonseparable. Since \\(\\ker Q_h=L\\), the open mapping theorem for \\(B/L\\to H_h\\) shows that\n\\[\nT(\\pi_h(a)\\xi_h)=\\pi_k(a)\\xi_k\n\\]\ndefines a bounded operator \\(T:H_h\\to H_k\\). It intertwines the representations. Thus \\(T^*T\\) is scalar by irreducibility of \\(\\pi_h\\); its value on \\(\\xi_h\\) shows that \\(T^*T=1\\). Taking vector coefficients gives \\(k=h\\).</p><p>We next construct the decreasing net. Order \\(N_+\\) by the usual operator order, a directed order, and set\n\\[\ne_s=1-(1+s)^{-1},\\qquad x_s=1-e_s\n\\quad(s\\in N_+).\n\\]\nFunctional calculus gives \\(e_s\\in N\\), and operator monotonicity of inversion makes \\((e_s)\\) increasing. For \\(b\\in N\\) and \\(s\\ge m(bb^*+b^*b)\\),\n\\[\n\\|(1-e_s)b\\|^2\n\\le \\frac1m\\|(1+s)^{-1}s(1+s)^{-1}\\|\n\\le\\frac1{4m};\n\\]\nthe same estimate holds for \\(\\|b(1-e_s)\\|^2\\). Thus \\((e_s)\\) is an approximate identity of \\(N\\), and \\(h(x_s)=1\\).</p><p>Let \\(q\\) be the strong limit of \\((e_s)\\), the support projection of \\(N\\). Every normal state supported on \\(1-q\\) annihilates \\(N\\), and hence is \\(h\\); conversely, \\(h(1-q)=1\\). The corner\n\\[\n(1-q)B^{**}(1-q)\n\\]\ntherefore has exactly one normal state. Normal states separate its self-adjoint elements, so this corner is scalar. It follows that \\(1-q=r_h\\) is minimal and \\(x_s\\downarrow r_h\\). The identity \\(r_har_h=h(a)r_h\\) follows by applying \\(h\\) to this scalar corner.</p><p>Finally, let \\(\\rho_s\\) be arbitrary states of \\(B\\). Every weak-star cluster point of the positive functionals\n\\[\na\\longmapsto \\rho_s(x_sax_s)\n\\]\nannihilates \\(N\\), by the approximate-identity estimates. A nonzero cluster point, after normalization, is therefore \\(h\\). For self-adjoint \\(a\\), a failure of (2.1) would give a subnet and norming states \\(\\rho_s\\) with a cluster point nonzero on \\(a-h(a)1\\), a contradiction. Real and imaginary parts give the conclusion for arbitrary \\(a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "projection excision", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(B\\) be a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra, and suppose decreasing projections \\((P_n)\\) in \\(B\\) peak to \\(h\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(B\\) be a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra, and suppose decreasing projections \\((P_n)\\) in \\(B\\) peak to \\(h\\). Then \\(P_n\\downarrow r_h\\) strongly and\n\\[\n\\|P_n(a-h(a)1)P_n\\|\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad(a\\in B).\n\\]\nIn particular, if \\(h(a)=0\\), then\n\\[\n\\|P_naP_t\\|\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\non the product index set.</p>", "proof": "<p>The infimum of the \\(P_n\\) in \\(B^{**}\\) supports exactly one normal state, namely \\(h\\). As in Lemma 1.4, it is the minimal projection \\(r_h\\). For arbitrary states \\(\\rho_n\\), every cluster point \\(\\psi\\) of \\(\\rho_n(P_n\\,\\cdot\\,P_n)\\) vanishes on \\(1-P_m\\) for each fixed \\(m\\). If \\(\\psi\\ne0\\), its normalization satisfies every peaking constraint and therefore equals \\(h\\). The same norming-state argument proves excision. For the last assertion use nesting:\n\\[\n\\|P_naP_t\\|\n\\le\\|P_{\\min(n,t)}aP_{\\min(n,t)}\\|.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "cross-excision", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(h\\not\\sim k\\) be pure states of a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\), and let \\((x_\\lambda)\\), \\((y_\\nu)\\) be decreasing nets as in Lemma 1.4 for \\(h\\), \\(k\\), respectively.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(h\\not\\sim k\\) be pure states of a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\), and let \\((x_\\lambda)\\), \\((y_\\nu)\\) be decreasing nets as in Lemma 1.4 for \\(h\\), \\(k\\), respectively. Then, for every \\(a\\in B\\),\n\\[\n\\|x_\\lambda ay_\\nu\\|\\longrightarrow0 \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\non the product directed set. Either net may be a peaking sequence of projections.</p>", "proof": "<p>The normal extension of \\(\\pi_h\\) annihilates \\(r_k\\). Otherwise a unit vector in the range of \\(\\pi_h^{**}(r_k)\\) would represent \\(k\\), by\n\\[\nr_kar_k=k(a)r_k.\n\\]\nEvery nonzero vector in an irreducible representation is cyclic, so this would imply \\(h\\sim k\\).</p><p>Consequently \\(h(ay_{\\nu_0}a^*)\\to0\\) as \\(\\nu_0\\) increases. For \\(\\nu\\ge\\nu_0\\), the inequalities \\(y_\\nu^2\\le y_\\nu\\le y_{\\nu_0}\\) give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|x_\\lambda ay_\\nu\\|^2\n&amp;\\le\\|x_\\lambda ay_{\\nu_0}a^*x_\\lambda\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le h(ay_{\\nu_0}a^*)\n+\\|x_\\lambda(ay_{\\nu_0}a^*-h(ay_{\\nu_0}a^*)1)x_\\lambda\\|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\nu_0\\) first, then use excision to choose \\(\\lambda_0\\). This proves smallness on a rectangular tail and hence the product-net limit. Lemma 1.5 gives the stated projection case.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.7", "orig": "trace-null supports", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\) has no finite-dimensional irreducible representations and \\(\\tau\\) is a tracial state on \\(B\\), then \\( \\tau(r_h)=0 \\) for every pure state \\(h\\) of \\(B\\).", "statement": "<p>If a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\) has no finite-dimensional irreducible representations and \\(\\tau\\) is a tracial state on \\(B\\), then\n\\[\n\\tau(r_h)=0\n\\]\nfor every pure state \\(h\\) of \\(B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The space \\(H_h\\) is infinite-dimensional. Choose orthonormal unit vectors \\(\\eta_j\\) and unitaries \\(u_j\\in B\\) with\n\\[\n\\pi_h(u_j)\\xi_h=\\eta_j,\n\\]\nas in Lemma 1.2. The projections \\(u_jr_hu_j^*\\) are pairwise orthogonal, since\n\\[\nr_hu_i^*u_jr_h=h(u_i^*u_j)r_h=0\n\\quad(i\\ne j).\n\\]\nThey all have trace \\(\\tau(r_h)\\), so \\(m\\tau(r_h)\\le1\\) for every positive integer \\(m\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Conditional expectations from peaking projections and preservation of simplicity:", "statement": "<p>**Conditional expectations from peaking projections and preservation of simplicity: paper Lemmas 2.8–2.10.**</p><p>A faithfully tracial algebra is a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra equipped with a faithful tracial state. A conditional expectation \\(E:C\\to B\\), for a unital inclusion \\(B\\subset C\\), is a unital completely positive map fixing \\(B\\) and satisfying\n\\[\nE(b_1ab_2)=b_1E(a)b_2\n\\quad(a\\in C,\\ b_1,b_2\\in B).\n\\]\nIt is faithful if \\(E(a^*a)=0\\) implies \\(a=0\\).</p><p>The following hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(B\\subset(C,\\tau)\\) is a unital subalgebra of a faithfully tracial algebra, there is at most one trace-preserving conditional expectation \\(C\\to B\\). If one exists, it is faithful.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Suppose \\((B,\\tau)\\) is faithfully tracial and decreasing projections \\((p_n)_{n\\ge0}\\) peak to \\(h\\), with \\(p_0=1\\) and trace values strictly decreasing to zero. Then\n\\[\nD=C^*(p_n:n\\ge0)\\cong C(\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}),\n\\]\nand a trace-preserving conditional expectation \\(E:B\\to D\\) exists. Writing \\(d_n=p_n-p_{n+1}\\), it is\n\\[\nE(a)(n)=\\frac{\\tau(d_nad_n)}{\\tau(d_n)},\n\\qquad E(a)(\\infty)=h(a). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(B\\subset C\\) has property \\((U)\\), \\(B\\) is simple, and there is a faithful conditional expectation \\(E:C\\to B\\), then \\(C\\) is simple.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 proves uniqueness and faithfulness of trace-preserving conditional expectations. Lemma 2.2 constructs the expectation associated with a peaking sequence. Lemma 2.3 proves preservation of simplicity under \\((U)\\) and a faithful conditional expectation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a unital subalgebra \\(B\\) of a faithfully tracial algebra \\((C,\\tau)\\), there is at most one trace-preserving conditional expectation \\(C\\to B\\).", "statement": "<p>For a unital subalgebra \\(B\\) of a faithfully tracial algebra \\((C,\\tau)\\), there is at most one trace-preserving conditional expectation \\(C\\to B\\). If one exists, it is faithful.</p>", "proof": "<p>For such an expectation, bimodularity gives\n\\[\n\\tau(b^*E(a))=\\tau(b^*a)\n\\quad(b\\in B).\n\\]\nTwo expectations therefore have difference \\(d\\in B\\) satisfying \\(\\tau(d^*d)=0\\), so they agree. Faithfulness follows from\n\\[\n\\tau(E(a^*a))=\\tau(a^*a).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\((B,\\tau)\\) be faithfully tracial.", "statement": "<p>Let \\((B,\\tau)\\) be faithfully tracial. Suppose decreasing projections \\((p_n)_{n\\ge0}\\) peak to \\(h\\), with \\(p_0=1\\) and trace values strictly decreasing to zero. Then\n\\[\nD=C^*(p_n:n\\ge0)\\cong C(\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}),\n\\]\nand there is a trace-preserving conditional expectation \\(E:B\\to D\\). Writing \\(d_n=p_n-p_{n+1}\\) for the finite-point atoms, it is given by (2.3).</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(d_n\\) are nonzero orthogonal projections. Their closed linear span is isomorphic to \\(c_0(\\mathbb N_0)\\), and adjoining the unit gives the claimed description of \\(D\\). Indeed,\n\\[\np_n=1-\\sum_{j&lt;n}d_j,\\qquad \\|p_n\\|=1,\n\\]\nsince \\(\\tau(p_n)&gt;0\\). Multiplying by \\(p_n\\) shows that \\(1\\) has distance at least one from every linear combination of \\(d_0,\\ldots,d_{n-1}\\).</p><p>The values in (2.3) define a continuous function: by \\(d_n\\le p_n\\) and projection excision from Proposition 1,\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{\\tau(d_nad_n)}{\\tau(d_n)}-h(a)\\right|\n\\le \\|p_n(a-h(a)1)p_n\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p><p>Every coordinate of \\(E\\) is a state, so \\(E\\) is unital and completely positive: positivity of each matrix amplification is tested pointwise in the commutative range. The map fixes \\(D\\). To check bimodularity, an element of \\(D\\) acts scalarly on each \\(d_n\\); at infinity, \\(h|_D\\) is the evaluation character. Cauchy–Schwarz for \\(h\\) then gives\n\\[\nh(ba)=h(b)h(a),\\qquad h(ab)=h(a)h(b)\n\\quad(b\\in D).\n\\]\nThese coordinate identities prove bimodularity.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\sum_n\\tau(d_n)=1,\n\\]\nand the trace on \\(D\\) has no mass at infinity. Summing the finite coordinates gives \\(\\tau(E(a))=\\tau(a)\\), since\n\\[\n|\\tau(p_Na)|\\le\\|a\\|\\tau(p_N)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose a unital inclusion \\(B\\subset C\\) satisfies \\((U)\\), \\(B\\) is simple, and there is a faithful conditional expectation \\(E:C\\to B\\).", "statement": "<p>Suppose a unital inclusion \\(B\\subset C\\) satisfies \\((U)\\), \\(B\\) is simple, and there is a faithful conditional expectation \\(E:C\\to B\\). Then \\(C\\) is simple.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(J\\) be a proper closed two-sided ideal of \\(C\\). The quotient map \\(q:C\\to C/J\\) is injective on \\(B\\), since \\(B\\) is simple and \\(q(1)\\ne0\\). Every pure state \\(h\\) of \\(B\\) therefore extends through this quotient to a state of \\(C\\) annihilating \\(J\\). By \\((U)\\), this extension is \\(h\\circ E\\). Thus\n\\[\nh(E(a))=0\n\\]\nfor every positive \\(a\\in J\\) and every pure \\(h\\) on \\(B\\). Pure states separate positive elements, so \\(E(a)=0\\); faithfulness gives \\(a=0\\). Hence \\(J=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "the reduced HNN adjunction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\((A,\\tau)\\) be a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra with faithful tracial state.", "statement": "<p>Let \\((A,\\tau)\\) be a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra with faithful tracial state. Let \\(D_1,D_2\\subset A\\) be unital subalgebras, let \\(E_i:A\\to D_i\\) be trace-preserving conditional expectations, and let \\(\\theta:D_1\\to D_2\\) be a trace-preserving isomorphism.</p><p>A word in \\(A\\) and a unitary \\(v\\) has the form\n\\[\na_0v^{\\epsilon_1}a_1\\cdots v^{\\epsilon_m}a_m,\n\\qquad a_t\\in A,\\quad \\epsilon_t\\in\\{+1,-1\\}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nFor \\(m\\ge1\\), call it reduced if\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nE_1(a_t)=0,&amp;(\\epsilon_t,\\epsilon_{t+1})=(+1,-1),\\\\\nE_2(a_t)=0,&amp;(\\epsilon_t,\\epsilon_{t+1})=(-1,+1).\n\\end{cases} \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nThere is no condition at equal consecutive signs.</p><p>There is a unital extension with a faithful tracial state extending \\(\\tau\\),\n\\[\nH(A)=C^*(A,v),\\qquad vdv^*=\\theta(d)\\quad(d\\in D_1),\n\\]\nwhere \\(v\\) is the stable unitary, and a trace-preserving conditional expectation\n\\[\nE_A:H(A)\\longrightarrow A\n\\]\nannihilating every nonempty reduced word.</p><p>Among faithfully tracial extensions generated by \\(A\\) and such a unitary, the relation and vanishing of the trace on nonempty reduced words determine \\(H(A)\\) uniquely, by an isomorphism fixing \\(A\\) and the named stable unitary.</p><p>The construction is the matrix corner\n\\[\nF=M_2(A),\\quad G=M_2(D_1),\\quad D=D_1\\oplus D_2,\n\\]\n\\[\nZ=F*_DG,\\qquad\ns=e_{21}^G,\\quad H(A)=e_1Ze_1,\\quad v=e_{12}^Fs, \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nwhere \\(D\\) embeds diagonally in \\(F\\) and by\n\\[\n(d_1,d_2)\\longmapsto\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\theta^{-1}(d_2))\n\\]\nin \\(G\\). Moreover, \\(Z\\) has dense linear span consisting of \\(F\\) and products\n\\[\na_0b_1a_1\\cdots b_ma_m,\\qquad\nm\\ge1,\\quad b_t\\in\\{s,s^*\\},\\quad a_t\\in F,\\quad\nE_D^F(a_t)=0\\ (0&lt;t&lt;m). \\tag{3.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Take\n\\[\nF=M_2(A),\\qquad G=M_2(D_1),\\qquad D=D_1\\oplus D_2.\n\\]\nEmbed \\(D\\) in \\(F\\) by \\((d_1,d_2)\\mapsto\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,d_2)\\) and in \\(G\\) by\n\\[\n(d_1,d_2)\\mapsto\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\theta^{-1}(d_2)).\n\\]\nThe normalized matrix traces agree on \\(D\\), and\n\\[\nE_D^F(a)=(E_1(a_{11}),E_2(a_{22})),\\qquad\nE_D^G(b)=(b_{11},\\theta(b_{22}))\n\\]\nare trace-preserving conditional expectations. Apply Lemma 3.1, retaining its module notation. The diagonal matrix projections \\(e_i=e_{ii}\\) are common to the two factors. Set\n\\[\ns=e_{21}^G,\\qquad H(A)=e_1Ze_1,\\qquad v=e_{12}^Fs,\n\\]\nand identify \\(A\\) with \\(e_1Fe_1\\). Then \\(v^*v=vv^*=e_1\\). For \\(d\\in D_1\\), the element \\(sds^*\\) is the second-corner copy of \\(\\theta(d)\\) in \\(F\\), so \\(vdv^*=\\theta(d)\\) in the first corner.</p><p>The matrix units of \\(F\\) identify \\(Z\\) with \\(M_2(e_1Ze_1)\\). Under this identification, the matrix algebra over \\(C^*(A,v)\\) contains \\(F\\) and \\(s=e_{21}^Fv\\). It contains \\(G\\) as well, because \\(G\\) is generated by \\(D\\) and \\(s,s^*\\). Hence \\(H(A)=C^*(A,v)\\). The trace on the corner is \\(2\\tau_Z\\), and its restriction to \\(A\\) is \\(\\tau\\). The restriction of \\(E_F\\) gives the required faithful trace-preserving expectation onto \\(A\\).</p><p>To check reduced words, write \\(q=e_{12}^F\\), so \\(v=qs\\) and \\(v^*=s^*q^*\\). Between consecutive \\(G\\)-letters the internal \\(F\\)-coefficients are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccc}\n(\\epsilon_t,\\epsilon_{t+1}) &amp;(+,+)&amp;(+,-)&amp;(-,+)&amp;(-,-)\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{coefficient}&amp;a_tq&amp;a_t&amp;q^*a_tq&amp;q^*a_t .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe first and last are off diagonal. The middle two have zero \\(D\\)-expectation precisely under the respective conditions in (3.2). Since \\(s,s^*\\) are centered, the expanded word is a product of the form (3.3), and its expectation onto \\(F\\), hence onto \\(A\\), is zero.</p><p>For uniqueness, split a coefficient at an opposite-sign turn as\n\\[\n(a-E_i(a))+E_i(a).\n\\]\nThe first term is centered at that turn, while the second cancels two stable letters using the unitary relation. Repeating this operation expresses every polynomial as an \\(A\\)-term plus nonempty reduced words. Thus the stipulated data determine the trace of every polynomial, and in particular all moments of \\(x^*x\\) for each polynomial \\(x\\).</p><p>For a faithful tracial state \\(t\\) on any \\(C^*\\)-algebra,\n\\[\n\\|x\\|=\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}t((x^*x)^r)^{1/(2r)}. \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nTo see this, restrict \\(t\\) to the commutative algebra generated by \\(x^*x\\) and the unit. Its spectral measure has full support, by faithfulness, and its \\(r\\)th-root moments tend to the spectral maximum. Consequently identical polynomial trace moments give identical polynomial norms. The generator correspondence is therefore isometric and extends to the asserted isomorphism. This argument requires no expectation in the comparison extension beyond the stated trace-vanishing rule.</p><p>Finally, every centered \\(G\\)-element is a sum of \\(D\\)-multiples of \\(s,s^*\\). Absorb these \\(D\\)-coefficients into neighboring \\(F\\)-coefficients; their internal centering is preserved by bimodularity. The spanning assertion (3.7) follows from Lemma 3.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "the reduced amalgamated free product", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(F,G\\) be unital \\(C^*\\)-algebras with faithful tracial states, containing a common unital subalgebra \\(D\\) on which the traces agree.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(F,G\\) be unital \\(C^*\\)-algebras with faithful tracial states, containing a common unital subalgebra \\(D\\) on which the traces agree. Suppose\n\\[\nE_D^F:F\\to D,\\qquad E_D^G:G\\to D\n\\]\nare trace-preserving conditional expectations.</p><p>There is a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(Z\\) generated by faithful copies of \\(F,G\\) agreeing on \\(D\\), with a faithful tracial state extending both traces and faithful trace-preserving conditional expectations\n\\[\nE_D:Z\\to D,\\qquad E_F:Z\\to F,\\qquad E_D=E_D^F\\circ E_F.\n\\]\nThe map \\(E_D\\) vanishes on every nonempty alternating product of elements of \\(\\ker E_D^F\\) and \\(\\ker E_D^G\\). The map \\(E_F\\) vanishes on every product\n\\[\na_0b_1a_1\\cdots b_ma_m,\\qquad m\\ge1, \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a_0,a_m\\in F\\), \\(b_t\\in\\ker E_D^G\\), and \\(a_t\\in\\ker E_D^F\\) for \\(0&lt;t&lt;m\\). The linear span of \\(F\\) and these products is dense in \\(Z\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(i\\in\\{F,G\\}\\), complete \\(i\\) as a right Hilbert \\(D\\)-module \\(L_i\\) with inner product\n\\[\n\\langle x,y\\rangle_D=E_D^i(x^*y).\n\\]\nLeft multiplication by \\(a\\in i\\) is bounded, since\n\\[\nE_D^i(x^*a^*ax)\\le\\|a\\|^2E_D^i(x^*x),\n\\]\nand its adjoint is left multiplication by \\(a^*\\). The expectation gives an orthogonal decomposition\n\\[\nL_i=D\\oplus L_i^\\circ,\\qquad L_i^\\circ=\\ker E_D^i.\n\\]\nBoth summands are invariant under left and right multiplication by \\(D\\). Form the Hilbert \\(D\\)-module\n\\[\nK=D\\oplus\n\\bigoplus_{\\substack{m\\ge1,\\ i_t\\in\\{F,G\\}\\\\i_t\\ne i_{t+1}}}\nL_{i_1}^\\circ\\otimes_D\\cdots\\otimes_D L_{i_m}^\\circ. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\otimes_D\\) is the interior tensor product of Hilbert modules; the left \\(D\\)-action on a word acts on its first factor. The first summand \\(D\\) is the vacuum summand, with distinguished vector \\(1\\).</p><p>Let \\(K^{(i)}\\) be the vacuum summand together with all words not starting with \\(i\\). The decomposition of \\(L_i\\) identifies\n\\[\nK=L_i\\otimes_DK^{(i)}.\n\\]\nRepresent \\(i\\) by left multiplication on the first tensor factor. The two representations agree on \\(D\\). They are faithful: an element \\(a\\) annihilating the vacuum satisfies \\(E_D^i(a^*a)=0\\), so its faithful factor trace gives \\(a=0\\). Let \\(Z\\) be the \\(C^*\\)-algebra they generate in the adjointable operators on \\(K\\).</p><p>A centered element of \\(i\\), meaning an element of \\(\\ker E_D^i\\), acting on \\(K^{(i)}\\) adds its own tensor factor at the front. Thus a nonempty alternating centered product has zero vacuum compression. Centering arbitrary factor elements shows that \\(D\\) and these products span a dense subspace of \\(Z\\). Vacuum compression consequently takes values in the left copy of \\(D\\) and defines the conditional expectation \\(E_D\\).</p><p>Write \\(\\tau_D\\) for the common trace on \\(D\\). We next prove that\n\\[\n\\tau_Z=\\tau_D\\circ E_D\n\\]\nis tracial and faithful. For traciality, compare \\(\\tau_Z(aw)\\) and \\(\\tau_Z(wa)\\) when \\(a\\) belongs to one factor and \\(w\\) is an alternating centered word. If \\(w\\) has length at least two, both values vanish. Indeed, multiplication at a same-factor end, followed by centering that end product, leaves either an alternating centered word or a \\(D\\)-coefficient times a nonempty such word. At a different-factor end, first center \\(a\\) and use \\(D\\)-bimodularity. For a one-letter word, use the factor trace when the factors agree; when they differ, both values are zero. For \\(w\\in D\\), use the factor trace. Density and repeated cyclic movement of a factor now prove traciality on all of \\(Z\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(\\tau_Z(z^*z)=0\\). For every \\(t\\in Z\\), traciality gives\n\\[\n0\\le\\tau_Z(t^*z^*zt)\n=\\tau_Z(ztt^*z^*)\n\\le\\|t\\|^2\\tau_Z(zz^*)=0.\n\\]\nThe trace on \\(D\\) is faithful, so\n\\[\nE_D((zt)^*(zt))=0.\n\\]\nThis is the \\(D\\)-valued inner product of \\(zt1\\) with itself, and hence \\(zt1=0\\). Products of centered letters create all elementary tensors in (3.4); thus \\(Z1\\) is dense in \\(K\\). It follows that \\(z=0\\), proving faithfulness of \\(\\tau_Z\\).</p><p>Finally, let \\(P_F\\) be the orthogonal projection onto\n\\[\nD\\oplus L_F^\\circ=L_F.\n\\]\nThis submodule reduces the \\(F\\)-action. A core\n\\[\nw=b_1a_1\\cdots a_{m-1}b_m\n\\]\nas in (3.3) sends \\(L_F\\) into words starting with \\(G\\), so \\(P_FwP_F=0\\). Since \\(P_F\\) commutes with \\(F\\), arbitrary endpoint coefficients satisfy\n\\[\nP_Fa_0wa_mP_F=a_0P_FwP_Fa_m=0.\n\\]\nSuccessive centering shows that \\(F\\) and the products (3.3) span a dense subspace. Their compressions belong to the faithful left copy of \\(F\\) on \\(L_F\\), which is norm closed. Compression therefore defines a conditional expectation \\(E_F:Z\\to F\\). The vacuum lies in \\(L_F\\), giving\n\\[\nE_D=E_D^F\\circ E_F\n\\]\nand trace preservation. Faithfulness follows from faithfulness of \\(\\tau_Z\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Restriction, interchange, and limits of reduced adjunctions:", "statement": "<p>**Restriction, interchange, and limits of reduced adjunctions: paper Corollary 3.3, Proposition 3.4, and Lemma 3.5.**</p><p>Use the reduced adjunction and reduced-word conventions of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. **Restriction.** Suppose \\((A,\\tau)\\), \\(D_i\\subset A\\), \\(E_i:A\\to D_i\\), and \\(\\theta:D_1\\to D_2\\) satisfy Proposition 3. If \\(A'\\subset A\\) is a unital subalgebra containing \\(D_1,D_2\\), then\n\\[\nC^*(A',v)\\subset H(A)\n\\]\nis the reduced adjunction \\(H(A')\\) for the restricted trace and expectations \\(E_i|_{A'}\\). The expectation \\(E_A\\) restricts to the expectation \\(H(A')\\to A'\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. **Interchange.** Let \\(O\\) be faithfully tracial. For \\(t\\in\\{j,k\\}\\), let\n\\[\nD_1^t,D_2^t\\subset O,\\qquad E_i^t:O\\to D_i^t,\\qquad\n\\theta^t:D_1^t\\to D_2^t\n\\]\nsatisfy Proposition 3. When forming an adjunction over \\(H_j(O)\\) or \\(H_k(O)\\), extend the other data expectations by composition with the expectation onto \\(O\\). Then there is a trace-preserving isomorphism\n\\[\nH_k(H_j(O))\\cong H_j(H_k(O)) \\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nfixing \\(O\\) and the named stable unitaries \\(v_j,v_k\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. **Limits.** Let \\(\\lambda\\) be a nonzero limit ordinal and \\((O_\\beta)_{\\beta&lt;\\lambda}\\) an increasing family of unital \\(C^*\\)-algebras with compatible faithful tracial states. Suppose fixed \\(D_1,D_2\\) lie in every \\(O_\\beta\\), with a fixed trace-preserving isomorphism \\(\\theta:D_1\\to D_2\\) and compatible trace-preserving conditional expectations\n\\[\nE_i^\\beta:O_\\beta\\to D_i.\n\\]\nIdentify the reduced adjunctions \\(H(O_\\beta)\\), by restriction, with the same stable unitary \\(v\\). With\n\\[\nO=\\overline{\\bigcup_{\\beta&lt;\\lambda}O_\\beta},\n\\qquad\nR=\\overline{\\bigcup_{\\beta&lt;\\lambda}H(O_\\beta)},\n\\]\nthe expectations extend to \\(O\\to D_i\\), and \\(R=H(O)\\) with its specified generators and trace.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 4.1–4.3 prove the three assertions. By Proposition 2, restrictions and compositions of the expectations agree whenever they have the same range and preserve the specified trace.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Corollary 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the restriction assumptions in Proposition 4, \\(C^*(A',v)\\subset H(A)\\) is \\(H(A')\\), and \\(E_A\\) restricts to its expectation onto \\(A'\\).", "statement": "<p>Under the restriction assumptions in Proposition 4, \\(C^*(A',v)\\subset H(A)\\) is \\(H(A')\\), and \\(E_A\\) restricts to its expectation onto \\(A'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Because \\(D_i\\subset A'\\), the restrictions \\(E_i|_{A'}\\) remain conditional expectations onto \\(D_i\\). The subalgebra \\(C^*(A',v)\\) inherits a faithful trace, the unitary relation, and the reduced-word trace vanishing. Proposition 3 identifies it with \\(H(A')\\). The reduction of polynomials in the proof of Proposition 3 shows that \\(E_A\\) maps this subalgebra into \\(A'\\) and has the stated restriction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Proposition 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the interchange assumptions in Proposition 4, there is a trace-preserving isomorphism \\( H_k(H_j(O))\\cong H_j(H_k(O)) \\) fixing \\(O,v_j,v_k\\).", "statement": "<p>Under the interchange assumptions in Proposition 4, there is a trace-preserving isomorphism\n\\[\nH_k(H_j(O))\\cong H_j(H_k(O))\n\\]\nfixing \\(O,v_j,v_k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Consider \\(C=H_k(H_j(O))\\). Lemma 4.1 places both \\(H_j(O)\\) and \\(H_k(O)\\) inside \\(C\\). Compose the expectations\n\\[\nC\\longrightarrow H_j(O)\\longrightarrow O,\n\\]\nand call the result \\(E_O\\). Its restriction to each smaller adjunction is that adjunction’s expectation onto \\(O\\), as can be checked on its reduced words.</p><p>We prove that the two smaller adjunctions are free over \\(O\\): an alternating product of elements from their respective kernels of \\(E_O\\) has zero \\(E_O\\)-expectation. Approximate each centered \\(H_k(O)\\)-element by finite sums of nonempty reduced \\(k\\)-words over \\(O\\). Such approximations follow by reducing a polynomial approximation and subtracting its \\(O\\)-expectation. Keep the centered \\(H_j(O)\\)-elements as coefficients.</p><p>When two \\(k\\)-words are joined across a centered element \\(b\\in H_j(O)\\), the new internal coefficient has the form \\(aba'\\), where \\(a,a'\\in O\\). Its expectation onto \\(O\\) is\n\\[\naE_O(b)a'=0,\n\\]\nso its expectation onto either \\(D_i^k\\) is also zero. The resulting word is therefore a nonempty reduced \\(k\\)-word over \\(H_j(O)\\), including at every new opposite-sign turn. Initial and final \\(H_j(O)\\)-coefficients are allowed. Its expectation onto \\(H_j(O)\\) vanishes. Passing to norm limits proves the freeness assertion. An alternating product with no \\(k\\)-block is a single centered \\(j\\)-element and is already killed by \\(E_O\\).</p><p>The individual expectations and this freeness determine all joint \\(O\\)-valued moments. Indeed, center the factors of a joint word; the fully alternating centered term vanishes, and every other term has fewer blocks after its \\(O\\)-coefficients are absorbed into neighbors. The resulting recursion is symmetric in \\(j,k\\). Applying the same argument in the reverse construction gives equal scalar trace moments for every polynomial in \\(O,v_j,v_k\\). Faithfulness and (3.6) give the desired isomorphism.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the limit assumptions in Proposition 4, the expectations extend to \\(O\\to D_i\\), and \\( \\overline{\\bigcup_{\\beta<\\lambda}H(O_\\beta)}=H(O) \\) with the specified generators and trace.", "statement": "<p>Under the limit assumptions in Proposition 4, the expectations extend to \\(O\\to D_i\\), and\n\\[\n\\overline{\\bigcup_{\\beta&lt;\\lambda}H(O_\\beta)}=H(O)\n\\]\nwith the specified generators and trace.</p>", "proof": "<p>The compatible norm-one expectations extend continuously to \\(O\\) and retain complete positivity, bimodularity, and trace preservation. Denote these extensions by \\(E_i\\). The compatible traces extend faithfully to both completions. For example, the quotient of \\(R\\) by its trace-null ideal is injective, hence isometric, on every earlier faithfully tracial algebra. It is therefore isometric on their dense union and on \\(R\\), so that ideal is zero. The same argument applies to \\(O\\).</p><p>We have \\(R=C^*(O,v)\\), and the unitary relation persists. Consider a nonempty reduced word with coefficients in \\(O\\). Approximate its finitely many coefficients in one common earlier \\(O_\\beta\\). At a turn requiring \\(E_i(a)=0\\), replace an approximation \\(b\\) by\n\\[\nb-E_i^\\beta(b).\n\\]\nThis remains in \\(O_\\beta\\), is exactly centered, and differs from \\(a\\) by at most twice the original error. Thus the given word is a norm limit of reduced words at earlier stages, all with trace zero. Proposition 3 identifies \\(R\\) with \\(H(O)\\). This finite-coefficient argument requires no assumption on the cofinality of \\(\\lambda\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "the bridge", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(A\\) be a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra with a faithful tracial state \\(\\tau\\) and no finite-dimensional irreducible representations.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A\\) be a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra with a faithful tracial state \\(\\tau\\) and no finite-dimensional irreducible representations. For \\(i=1,2\\), let \\((p_n^i)_{n\\ge0}\\) be a decreasing sequence of projections, with \\(p_0^i=1\\), peaking to a pure state \\(f_i\\). Suppose\n\\[\nf_1\\not\\sim f_2,\\qquad\n\\tau(p_n^1)=\\tau(p_n^2)=t_n,\n\\]\n\\[\n1=t_0&gt;t_1&gt;\\cdots&gt;0,\\qquad t_n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nD_i=C^*(p_n^i:n\\ge0),\\qquad \\theta(p_n^1)=p_n^2.\n\\]\nForm the reduced HNN extension \\(H(A)\\) of Proposition 3 using these data and the trace-preserving conditional expectations of Proposition 2.</p><p>Every pure state \\(h\\) of \\(A\\) has a unique state extension \\(\\widetilde h\\) to \\(H(A)\\). These extensions are pure, and, for all pure states \\(h,k\\) of \\(A\\),\n\\[\n\\widetilde h\\sim\\widetilde k\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nh\\sim k\n\\ \\text{or}\\\n\\{[h],[k]\\}=\\{[f_1],[f_2]\\}.\n\\]\nThe extension has a faithful tracial state extending \\(\\tau\\) and a faithful trace-preserving conditional expectation onto \\(A\\). If \\(A\\) is simple, then \\(H(A)\\) is simple.</p><p>For the proof, use the matrix-corner notation\n\\[\nF=M_2(A),\\qquad G=M_2(D_1),\\qquad D=D_1\\oplus D_2,\\qquad Z=F*_DG,\n\\]\n\\[\ne_i=e_{ii},\\qquad s=e_{21}^G,\\qquad\nP_n^i=p_n^ie_i,\\qquad\n\\varphi_i(a)=f_i(a_{ii})\\quad(a\\in F).\n\\]\nThe notation \\(p_n^ie_i\\) means the matrix with entry \\(p_n^i\\) in position \\((i,i)\\) and zero elsewhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 2 supplies the expectations onto \\(D_i\\). The differences \\(p_n^i-p_{n+1}^i\\) are nonzero, mutually orthogonal projections, so each \\(D_i\\) is the algebra of continuous functions on \\(\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\). Matching these differences defines \\(\\theta\\), and equality of trace values makes it trace preserving. Thus Proposition 3 applies. Its trace and expectation give the corresponding assertions of the proposition.</p><p>We prove unique extension and exclusion of unintended joins for \\(F\\subset Z\\), then return to the corner. By unitary conjugacy from Proposition 1, it suffices to use \\(\\varphi_i\\) as representative of its distinguished equivalence class: conjugating by a unitary of \\(F\\) bijects the sets of state extensions.</p><p>In the equal-state test of Lemma 5.1, let \\(\\psi\\) be any state of \\(Z\\) extending \\(h\\). Its GNS vector is fixed by every \\(x_\\lambda,y_\\nu\\): a positive contraction \\(x\\) of state value one satisfies\n\\[\n\\|(1-\\pi_\\psi(x))\\xi_\\psi\\|^2\n\\le\\psi(1-x)=0.\n\\]\nConsequently, for every nonempty word \\(W\\) in (3.7),\n\\[\n\\psi(W)=\\psi(x_\\lambda Wy_\\nu)\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nby Lemma 5.1. Its values on \\(F\\) are already prescribed. The dense span (3.7) therefore proves uniqueness; existence also follows directly by composing \\(h\\) with the expectation onto \\(F\\). Conjugacy by unitaries in \\(F\\) establishes uniqueness for every old pure state, including all states in the two distinguished classes. Their unique extensions are pure by Proposition 1.</p><p>Next take an inequivalent pair allowed in (4.1). If its unique lifts were equivalent, realize them by unit vectors \\(\\xi_h,\\xi_k\\) in one irreducible representation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(Z\\). The represented excision elements fix the corresponding vectors, so\n\\[\n\\langle\\pi(z)\\xi_k,\\xi_h\\rangle\n=\n\\langle\\pi(x_\\lambda zy_\\nu)\\xi_k,\\xi_h\\rangle.\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\in F\\), the right side tends to zero by cross-excision from Proposition 1; for a tested nonempty word it tends to zero by Lemma 5.1. Thus this matrix coefficient vanishes on all of \\(Z\\), contradicting cyclicity of \\(\\xi_k\\). Unitary conjugacy within the old classes extends this exclusion to all representatives. Equivalent old states have equivalent unique lifts by Proposition 1.</p><p>States of \\(A=e_1Fe_1\\) identify with states of \\(F\\) taking value one on \\(e_1\\), by composition with compression. The same correspondence holds between \\(H(A)=e_1Ze_1\\) and \\(Z\\), and commutes with restriction. Every extension to \\(Z\\) of an \\(e_1\\)-supported state of \\(F\\) is still \\(e_1\\)-supported. Hence this correspondence bijects extension sets and transfers unique extension to \\(A\\subset H(A)\\).</p><p>Under \\(F=M_2(A)\\) and \\(Z=M_2(H(A))\\), these supported states have the matrix amplifications of their corner GNS representations, with representing vector in the first summand. Purity and equivalence of classes are therefore preserved. The matrix-flip unitary of \\(F\\) moves \\(\\varphi_2\\) from the second summand to the first, so its class corresponds to \\([f_2]\\), just as \\([\\varphi_1]\\) corresponds to \\([f_1]\\). The exclusion of all other joins descends to the corner.</p><p>For the designated join, the stable unitary relation shows that\n\\[\n\\widetilde f_2\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(v)\n\\]\ntakes value one on every \\(p_n^1\\), so its restriction to \\(A\\) is \\(f_1\\). Unique extension gives\n\\[\n\\widetilde f_1=\\widetilde f_2\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}(v).\n\\]\nThis proves the equivalence criterion. If \\(A\\) is simple, Proposition 2 applies to the faithful expectation and \\((U)\\), proving that \\(H(A)\\) is simple.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The unnumbered reduced-word norm estimate in the proof of paper Theorem 4.1, equation (4.2).** In the matrix-corner setting of Proposition 5, consider pure states \\(h,k\\) of \\(F\\) satisfying one of the two tests …", "statement": "<p>**The unnumbered reduced-word norm estimate in the proof of paper Theorem 4.1, equation (4.2).**</p><p>In the matrix-corner setting of Proposition 5, consider pure states \\(h,k\\) of \\(F\\) satisfying one of the two tests\n\\[\nh=k,\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nh\\not\\sim k\n\\ \\text{and}\\\n\\{[h],[k]\\}\\ne\\{[\\varphi_1],[\\varphi_2]\\}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nWhenever either state belongs to \\([\\varphi_i]\\), take it to be \\(\\varphi_i\\) itself.</p><p>Choose decreasing positive contractive excision nets \\(x=x_\\lambda\\) for \\(h\\) and \\(y=y_\\nu\\) for \\(k\\), as in Proposition 1; at \\(\\varphi_i\\), choose the peaking sequence \\(P_n^i\\). Then\n\\[\n\\|xa_0b_1a_1\\cdots b_ma_my\\|\\longrightarrow0 \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nfor every \\(m\\ge1\\), every \\(b_j\\in\\{s,s^*\\}\\), and every \\(a_j\\in F\\) with\n\\[\nE_D^F(a_j)=0\\qquad(0&lt;j&lt;m). \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nAll limits involving both endpoints are on the product of their index sets.</p>", "proof": "<p>The projections \\(P_n^i\\) peak to \\(\\varphi_i\\): their zeroth term is \\(e_i\\), so a state taking value one on them is supported on that corner and restricts there to \\(f_i\\). The states \\(\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2\\) are pure and inequivalent, by matrix amplification of the GNS representations of \\(f_1,f_2\\).</p><p>Also \\(F\\) has no finite-dimensional irreducible representations. Indeed, any such representation would restrict along \\(a\\mapsto\\operatorname{diag}(a,a)\\) to a nonzero finite-dimensional representation of \\(A\\), whose image would supply a finite-dimensional irreducible representation of \\(A\\).</p><p>Let \\(P_G\\) be the orthogonal projection of the reduced free-product Hilbert \\(D\\)-module \\(K\\) onto the summands starting with a factor in \\(L_G^\\circ\\). For \\(d\\in F\\), the decomposition \\(K=L_F\\otimes_DK^{(F)}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|dP_G\\|\\le\\|E_D^F(d^*d)\\|^{1/2}. \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nIndeed, a vector \\(\\eta\\in P_GK\\) has the form \\(1\\otimes\\eta\\) in this decomposition, and\n\\[\n\\|d\\otimes\\eta\\|^2\n=\\|\\langle\\eta,E_D^F(d^*d)\\eta\\rangle\\|\n\\le\\|E_D^F(d^*d)\\|\\,\\|\\eta\\|^2.\n\\]\nThe expectation norm here is the norm in \\(D\\).</p><p>For \\(b\\in\\{s,s^*\\}\\), write\n\\[\nb=e_{\\ell(b)}be_{r(b)}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n(\\ell(s),r(s))=(2,1),\\qquad\n(\\ell(s^*),r(s^*))=(1,2).\n\\]\nFor a word in (4.2), put\n\\[\n\\ell=\\ell(b_1),\\qquad r=r(b_m),\\qquad\nw=b_1a_1\\cdots a_{m-1}b_m.\n\\]\nEach letter of this centered alternating core creates a new leg when applied, from right to left, to a word not starting with \\(G\\). Therefore\n\\[\n(1-P_G)w(1-P_G)=0,\\qquad\nw=P_Gw+(1-P_G)wP_G.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nd=xa_0e_\\ell,\\qquad d'=e_ra_my.\n\\]\nWe obtain\n\\[\n\\|dwd'\\|\n\\le\n\\|dP_G\\|\\,\\|w\\|\\,\\|d'\\|\n+\\|d\\|\\,\\|w\\|\\,\\|P_Gd'\\|. \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nUsing (4.4) and its adjoint version, it follows that (4.2) holds whenever both\n\\[\n\\|E_D^F(e_\\ell a_0^*x^2a_0e_\\ell)\\|\\longrightarrow0,\n\\qquad\n\\|E_D^F(e_ra_my^2a_m^*e_r)\\|\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nThis sufficient criterion does not require the pair of endpoint states to satisfy (4.1).</p><p>**When an endpoint decays.** In the first line of (4.6), increase \\(x^2\\) to \\(x\\). The resulting elements are a decreasing net of positive continuous functions on the \\(\\ell\\)th convergent sequence in the spectrum of \\(D\\); they vanish on the other component. At a finite atom\n\\[\nQ=P_n^\\ell-P_{n+1}^\\ell,\n\\]\ntheir pointwise limit is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\tau_F(Qa_0^*r_ha_0Q)}{\\tau_F(Q)}=0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\tau_F\\) is the normalized matrix trace, extended normally to \\(F^{**}\\). The numerator vanishes by traciality and trace-null supports from Proposition 1; for example,\n\\[\n0\\le\\tau_F(r_ha_0Qa_0^*)\n\\le\\|a_0Qa_0^*\\|\\tau_F(r_h)=0.\n\\]</p><p>At the point \\(\\infty\\), the limit is \\(\\varphi_\\ell(a_0^*r_ha_0)\\). It is zero if \\(h\\not\\sim\\varphi_\\ell\\), because the normal extension of the \\(\\varphi_\\ell\\)-representation annihilates \\(r_h\\), as in cross-excision. If \\(h=\\varphi_\\ell\\), the support identity instead gives\n\\[\n\\varphi_\\ell(a_0^*r_ha_0)=|\\varphi_\\ell(a_0)|^2.\n\\]\nThus the first endpoint decays unless \\(h=\\varphi_\\ell\\), and it still decays in that case when \\(\\varphi_\\ell(a_0)=0\\).</p><p>The uniform convergence used here is Dini’s theorem for monotone nets [9, Theorem 1.5, p. 306]. For fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), the open sets where a chosen function is below \\(\\varepsilon\\) cover the compact spectrum. Choose a finite subcover and an index dominating its finitely many indices. Monotonicity makes every later function uniformly smaller than \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>The second line of (4.6) is symmetric: it decays unless \\(k=\\varphi_r\\), and also decays in that case when \\(\\varphi_r(a_m)=0\\).</p><p>**Remove the matched endpoints.** Call the left endpoint matched when \\(h=\\varphi_\\ell\\), and the right endpoint matched when \\(k=\\varphi_r\\). At each matched endpoint write\n\\[\na_0=\\varphi_\\ell(a_0)1+a_0^0,\n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\na_m=\\varphi_r(a_m)1+a_m^0.\n\\]\nExpand the word using these decompositions. In each resulting term, an unmatched endpoint already satisfies its estimate in (4.6), and a matched endpoint with its zero-value coefficient does as well. Treat the scalar terms by moving the corresponding peak through its adjacent letter:\n\\[\nP_n^{\\ell(b)}b=bP_n^{r(b)},\\qquad\nbP_n^{r(b)}=P_n^{\\ell(b)}b. \\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nThese identities hold in \\(G\\): the diagonal embedding of \\(D_2\\) uses \\(\\theta^{-1}\\), so the two projections have the same matrix entry \\(p_n^1\\) there.</p><p>In the following displays, an untransferred endpoint coefficient means the coefficient chosen in the expansion. For \\(m\\ge2\\), after suppressing its scalar coefficient, a left scalar term becomes\n\\[\nP_n^{\\ell(b_1)}b_1a_1\\cdots b_ma_my\n=\nb_1\\bigl(P_n^{r(b_1)}a_1b_2\\cdots b_ma_my\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe peak now belongs to the other distinguished state, and \\(b_1\\) is a bounded outside factor. A right scalar choice similarly moves \\(P_t^{r(b_m)}\\) to \\(P_t^{\\ell(b_m)}\\) and leaves \\(b_m\\) as a bounded factor on the right. When both ends are transferred and \\(m\\ge3\\), the resulting expression, again up to its scalar coefficient, is\n\\[\nb_1\\bigl(\nP_n^{r(b_1)}a_1b_2\\cdots b_{m-1}a_{m-1}P_t^{\\ell(b_m)}\n\\bigr)b_m.\n\\]</p><p>Suppose at least one core letter remains after the transfers. Each newly exposed endpoint coefficient is one of the original internal \\(a_j\\). By (4.3),\n\\[\n\\varphi_1(a_j)=\\varphi_2(a_j)=0:\n\\]\nthese states evaluate the two components of \\(E_D^F(a_j)\\) at \\(\\infty\\). Thus every new endpoint satisfies its estimate, whether or not its transferred state matches the adjacent letter. Endpoints that were not transferred satisfy their estimates by the preceding expansion. Applying (4.6) to the remaining word therefore proves decay of each such term. This covers no transfers, one transfer when \\(m\\ge2\\), and two transfers when \\(m\\ge3\\), including a remaining core of one letter. The sufficient endpoint criterion requires no assumption that the transferred pair of states satisfies (4.1).</p><p>It remains to check the cases in which the core disappears. If \\(m=1\\), both ends cannot match: the range and source of \\(s\\) or \\(s^*\\) are different, so double matching would be the designated pair excluded in (4.1). For a single left transfer, say \\(h=\\varphi_i\\), the remaining compression, up to a scalar and a bounded outside letter, is\n\\[\nP_n^ja_1y_\\nu,\\qquad j\\ne i.\n\\]\nThe state \\(k\\) is inequivalent to \\(\\varphi_j\\). In the equal-state test it equals \\(\\varphi_i\\); in the other test, equivalence to \\(\\varphi_j\\) would again be the excluded designated pair. Hence this expression tends to zero by cross-excision from Proposition 1. A single right transfer is symmetric.</p><p>Finally, let \\(m=2\\) and transfer at both ends. Both original endpoints matched, so (4.1) forces\n\\[\nh=k=\\varphi_i\n\\]\nfor some \\(i\\). Since each letter switches the two matrix indices,\n\\[\n\\ell(b_1)=r(b_2)=i,\\qquad\nr(b_1)=\\ell(b_2)=j\\ne i.\n\\]\nThe remaining compression is \\(P_n^ja_1P_t^j\\), with \\(\\varphi_j(a_1)=0\\). If \\(q=\\min(n,t)\\), nestedness and excision give\n\\[\n\\|P_n^ja_1P_t^j\\|\n\\le\\|P_q^ja_1P_q^j\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThis is convergence on the product of the two sequence indices. Together with cross-excision, whose conclusion also holds on the product net, these cases prove (4.2) in full.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Permanence along continuous chains:", "statement": "<p>**Permanence along continuous chains: paper Lemmas 5.1–5.2 and the accompanying permanence observations.**</p><p>A chain \\((B_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\le\\lambda}\\) of unital inclusions is continuous if\n\\[\nB_\\gamma=\\overline{\\bigcup_{\\alpha&lt;\\gamma}B_\\alpha}\n\\]\nat every nonzero limit ordinal \\(\\gamma\\le\\lambda\\).</p><p>The following hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If every successor inclusion in a continuous chain has \\((U)\\), then every inclusion \\(B_\\alpha\\subset B_\\beta\\), \\(\\alpha\\le\\beta\\le\\lambda\\), has \\((U)\\). Unique lifts of equivalent pure states are equivalent.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(\\lambda\\) is a nonzero limit ordinal and pure states \\(h,k\\) of \\(B_\\lambda\\) restrict to inequivalent pure states of \\(B_\\alpha\\) for all \\(\\alpha\\) in some tail below \\(\\lambda\\), then \\(h\\not\\sim k\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The closure of a continuous increasing union of simple unital \\(C^*\\)-algebras is simple. Compatible faithful tracial states remain faithful at the limit. At successors, \\((U)\\) and a faithful conditional expectation preserve simplicity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 6.1–6.2 give the assertions about states.</p><p>A continuous union of simple unital algebras is simple: a proper quotient is injective, hence isometric, on every earlier algebra, so it is isometric on their dense union and its completion.</p><p>Compatible faithful tracial states remain faithful at the limit. The extended trace’s null ideal has zero intersection with every earlier algebra; the quotient by this ideal is again isometric on a dense union, so the ideal is zero. At successors, simplicity follows from Proposition 2 whenever \\((U)\\) and a faithful conditional expectation are available.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\((B_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\le\\lambda}\\) be a continuous chain whose successor inclusions have property \\((U)\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\((B_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\le\\lambda}\\) be a continuous chain whose successor inclusions have property \\((U)\\). Then every inclusion \\(B_\\alpha\\subset B_\\beta\\), for \\(\\alpha\\le\\beta\\le\\lambda\\), has \\((U)\\). The unique lifts of equivalent pure states are equivalent.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a successor, unique extensions compose, and the intermediate lift is pure by Proposition 1. At a limit, two extensions agree on each earlier algebra, hence on their dense union. Existence follows from state extension. Transfinite induction gives the first assertion.</p><p>For the second, a unitary implementing equivalence in the original algebra also conjugates the unique lifts, by Proposition 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\((B_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\le\\lambda}\\) be continuous, with \\(\\lambda\\) a nonzero limit ordinal.", "statement": "<p>Let \\((B_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\le\\lambda}\\) be continuous, with \\(\\lambda\\) a nonzero limit ordinal. Suppose pure states \\(h,k\\) of \\(B_\\lambda\\) restrict to inequivalent pure states of \\(B_\\alpha\\) for all \\(\\alpha\\) in some tail below \\(\\lambda\\). Then \\(h\\not\\sim k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Otherwise realize \\(h,k\\) by unit vectors \\(\\xi,\\eta\\) in one irreducible representation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(B_\\lambda\\). For each earlier \\(\\alpha\\) in the given tail, the cyclic subspaces\n\\[\nM_\\alpha=\\overline{\\pi(B_\\alpha)\\xi},\n\\qquad\nN_\\alpha=\\overline{\\pi(B_\\alpha)\\eta}\n\\]\nare reducing and carry inequivalent irreducible representations of \\(B_\\alpha\\). The orthogonal projection onto \\(N_\\alpha\\), restricted to \\(M_\\alpha\\), is an intertwiner, so it is zero. Thus\n\\[\n\\langle\\pi(a)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=0\n\\quad(a\\in B_\\alpha).\n\\]\nContinuity gives the same equality for every \\(a\\in B_\\lambda\\), contradicting cyclicity of \\(\\xi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The initial algebra and inherited subiterations:", "statement": "<p>**The initial algebra and inherited subiterations: paper Lemmas 5.3–5.4.**</p><p>Put\n\\[\nA_0=\\bigotimes_{m=1}^{\\infty}M_2,\\qquad\np_n=e_{11}^{\\otimes n}\\otimes1\\quad(n\\ge1),\\qquad p_0=1.\n\\]\nHere \\(A_0\\) is the norm closure of its unital matrix subalgebras \\(M_{2^n}\\), with their compatible normalized matrix traces.</p><p>The algebra \\(A_0\\) is separable, simple, and infinite-dimensional, its trace is faithful, and \\((p_n)\\) peaks to a pure state \\(f_0\\), with\n\\[\n\\tau(p_n)=2^{-n}.\n\\]\nEvery unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra containing \\(A_0\\) has no finite-dimensional irreducible representations.</p><p>Consider a continuous chain \\((X_\\beta)_{\\beta\\le\\delta}\\) starting at \\(X_0=A_0\\). At an active index \\(j&lt;\\delta\\), perform the bridge of Proposition 5 using \\((p_n)\\) and decreasing projections \\((q_n^j)\\) that peak in \\(X_j\\) to a state inequivalent to the \\(p_n\\)-state, with\n\\[\nq_0^j=1,\\qquad \\tau(q_n^j)=2^{-n}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(v_j\\) for its stable unitary:\n\\[\nX_{j+1}=C^*(X_j,v_j).\n\\]\nAt every other index set \\(X_{j+1}=X_j\\). Use the compatible traces supplied by the bridges.</p><p>For \\(T\\subset\\delta\\), define\n\\[\nA[T]=C^*(A_0,\\{v_j:j\\in T,\\ j\\text{ active}\\})\\subset X_\\delta.\n\\]\nThus \\(A[\\beta]=X_\\beta\\) when \\(\\beta\\) is regarded as the set of smaller ordinals.</p><p>Choose countable sets \\(I_j\\subset j\\) such that \\(q_n^j\\in A[I_j]\\) for every \\(n\\) at each active \\(j\\), and set \\(I_j=\\varnothing\\) otherwise. Call \\(T\\) dependency-closed if\n\\[\nj\\in T\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad I_j\\subset T. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]</p><p>Every element of \\(X_\\delta\\) belongs to \\(A[T]\\) for some countable \\(T\\). Every countable set of indices is contained in a countable dependency-closed set.</p><p>For every dependency-closed \\(T\\subset\\delta\\), the algebras\n\\[\nA[T\\cap\\beta],\\qquad \\beta\\le\\delta,\n\\]\nform a continuous bridge iteration in the inherited order, with identity steps at indices outside \\(T\\). In particular, \\(A[T]\\) is simple and has a faithful tracial state. If \\(T\\) is countable, \\(A[T]\\) is separable.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 proves the assertions about \\(A_0\\).</p><p>By Proposition 6, \\((p_n)\\) peaks at every stage. For each active \\(j\\), \\((q_n^j)\\) peaks at every stage from \\(j\\) onward, and its state is equivalent to the \\(p_n\\)-state from stage \\(j+1\\) onward.</p><p>Every element of \\(X_\\delta\\) lies in \\(A[T]\\) for some countable \\(T\\): approximate it by a sequence of polynomials, each involving finitely many stable unitaries. This also permits the choice of the countable dependency sets \\(I_j\\). Every countable set is contained in a countable dependency-closed set: repeatedly adjoin the dependencies of its members and take the union over the natural numbers.</p><p>We prove the inherited-iteration assertion, which is paper Lemma 5.4. Let \\(k\\in T\\) be active. Both sequences for its bridge lie in \\(A[T\\cap k]\\), by (5.1). The original expectations onto their generated abelian algebras restrict to this smaller base, since their ranges already lie in it. The restriction assertion of Proposition 4 identifies the algebra obtained by adjoining \\(v_k\\) with that reduced HNN extension.</p><p>The sequences peak to pure states of the smaller base by Proposition 1, applied to its inclusion in \\(X_k\\). The same peaking-under-restriction assertion gives each of these particular states its unique extension to \\(X_k\\). If the two smaller states were equivalent, unitary conjugacy and uniqueness from Proposition 1 would make their extensions equivalent, contrary to the choice of the original active step. The smaller base contains \\(A_0\\), so Lemma 7.1 excludes finite-dimensional irreducibles. All hypotheses of Proposition 5 therefore hold in the inherited iteration.</p><p>Continuity follows because a polynomial uses only finitely many indices. Simplicity follows by induction from Propositions 2 and 6. Faithfulness follows either from the restricted trace on \\(X_\\delta\\) or from Proposition 6. Finally, a countable set of stable unitaries together with the separable algebra \\(A_0\\) generates a separable algebra.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the algebra \\(A_0\\) and projections \\(p_n\\) in Proposition 7, \\(A_0\\) is separable, simple, and infinite-dimensional, its trace is faithful, and \\((p_n)\\) peaks to a pure state \\(f_0\\).", "statement": "<p>For the algebra \\(A_0\\) and projections \\(p_n\\) in Proposition 7, \\(A_0\\) is separable, simple, and infinite-dimensional, its trace is faithful, and \\((p_n)\\) peaks to a pure state \\(f_0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\tau(p_n)=2^{-n}.\n\\]\nEvery unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra containing \\(A_0\\) has no finite-dimensional irreducible representations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Simplicity and faithfulness follow from Proposition 6 applied to the matrix stages. Separability and infinite dimension are immediate from those stages. A state taking value one on \\(p_n\\) must restrict to the vector state of the first basis vector on \\(M_{2^n}\\). These vector states are compatible, so they determine exactly one state on the dense union and its completion. This proves peaking and purity. The trace formula is the normalized matrix trace formula.</p><p>A nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible representation of an algebra containing \\(A_0\\) restricts to a unital representation of \\(A_0\\). Its kernel is zero by simplicity, which would embed the infinite-dimensional algebra \\(A_0\\) in a finite-dimensional algebra.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "closed subiterations have unique extensions", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\( A_0=\\bigotimes_{m=1}^{\\infty}M_2,\\qquad p_0=1,\\qquad p_n=e_{11}^{\\otimes n}\\otimes1\\quad(n\\ge1). \\) Let \\((X_\\beta)_{\\beta\\le\\delta}\\) be a continuous bridge iteration starting at \\(A_0\\).", "statement": "<p>Let\n\\[\nA_0=\\bigotimes_{m=1}^{\\infty}M_2,\\qquad\np_0=1,\\qquad p_n=e_{11}^{\\otimes n}\\otimes1\\quad(n\\ge1).\n\\]\nLet \\((X_\\beta)_{\\beta\\le\\delta}\\) be a continuous bridge iteration starting at \\(A_0\\). At each active \\(j&lt;\\delta\\), its bridge joins the state peaked by \\((p_n)\\) to an inequivalent state peaked in \\(X_j\\) by decreasing projections \\((q_n^j)\\), where\n\\[\nq_0^j=1,\\qquad \\tau(q_n^j)=2^{-n},\n\\qquad X_{j+1}=C^*(X_j,v_j).\n\\]\nOther successor steps are identities.</p><p>Define\n\\[\nA[T]=C^*(A_0,\\{v_j:j\\in T,\\ j\\text{ active}\\}),\n\\]\nand choose countable \\(I_j\\subset j\\) containing the supports of all \\(q_n^j\\), in the sense that \\(q_n^j\\in A[I_j]\\); put \\(I_j=\\varnothing\\) at inactive indices. A set \\(T\\subset\\delta\\) is dependency-closed if \\(j\\in T\\) implies \\(I_j\\subset T\\).</p><p>For every dependency-closed \\(S\\subset\\delta\\), the inclusion\n\\[\nA[S]\\subset X_\\delta\n\\]\nhas property \\((U)\\).</p><p>For the proof, if \\(j\\notin S\\) is active and \\(j+1\\le\\beta\\le\\delta\\), put\n\\[\nO_\\beta=A[j\\cup(S\\cap\\beta)],\\qquad\nR_\\beta=A[(j+1)\\cup(S\\cap\\beta)]. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nHere the ordinal \\(j\\) is the set of indices below \\(j\\), and \\(j+1\\) also contains \\(j\\). Write \\(H_j\\) for the reduced HNN adjunction with fixed data \\((p_n),(q_n^j)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Insert the omitted indices, in their original order, after \\(A[S]\\).</p><p>Fix an active \\(j\\notin S\\). At \\(\\beta=\\delta\\),\n\\[\nO_\\delta=A[S\\cup j],\\qquad\nR_\\delta=A[S\\cup(j+1)].\n\\]\nBy Lemma 8.1, the two sequences still peak to inequivalent states on \\(O_\\delta\\). By Lemma 8.2,\n\\[\nR_\\delta\\cong H_j(O_\\delta).\n\\]\nThus Proposition 5 applies and gives \\((U)\\) for\n\\[\nA[S\\cup j]\\subset A[S\\cup(j+1)].\n\\]\nIf \\(j\\in S\\) or \\(j\\) is inactive, this inclusion is the identity.</p><p>The continuous chain\n\\[\n(A[S\\cup j])_{j\\le\\delta}\n\\]\nstarts at \\(A[S]\\) and ends at \\(X_\\delta\\). Continuity follows because a polynomial involves only finitely many indices outside \\(S\\). Proposition 6 now proves \\((U)\\) for \\(A[S]\\subset X_\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**First unnumbered claim in the proof of paper Theorem 5.5:", "statement": "<p>**First unnumbered claim in the proof of paper Theorem 5.5: the omitted pair stays inequivalent.**</p><p>Under Proposition 8, fix an active \\(j\\notin S\\). For every \\(j+1\\le\\beta\\le\\delta\\), the sequences \\((p_n)\\) and \\((q_n^j)\\) peak to inequivalent pure states on\n\\[\nO_\\beta=A[j\\cup(S\\cap\\beta)].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The index sets defining \\(O_\\beta,R_\\beta\\) are dependency-closed. Initially,\n\\[\nO_{j+1}=X_j,\\qquad R_{j+1}=X_{j+1};\n\\]\nafterward both chains perform precisely the inherited steps with indices in \\(S\\), by Proposition 7.</p><p>Both \\((p_n)\\) and \\((q_n^j)\\) peak on every \\(O_\\beta\\) by Proposition 1, since they peak in \\(X_\\delta\\). Their states are initially inequivalent.</p><p>Suppose \\(k&gt;j\\) is an active member of \\(S\\), and they are still inequivalent on \\(O_k\\). The \\(q_n^k\\)-sequence also lies in \\(O_k\\) and peaks there. Each of these three particular states has a unique extension to \\(X_k\\), by peaking under restriction from Proposition 1; this does not use Proposition 8.</p><p>In \\(X_k\\), the \\(q_n^j\\)-state is equivalent to the \\(p_n\\)-state, because step \\(j\\) has already been performed. The \\(q_n^k\\)-state is inequivalent to that state, because step \\(k\\) is active. Equivalence on \\(O_k\\) would pass to these particular unique extensions. Consequently, on \\(O_k\\), the \\(q_n^k\\)-state is equivalent to neither the \\(p_n\\)-state nor the \\(q_n^j\\)-state.</p><p>The \\(k\\)-bridge joins only the classes of its designated pair, by Proposition 5. It therefore does not join the two states of the omitted \\(j\\)-bridge. At limits their inequivalence persists by Proposition 6. Transfinite induction proves the claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Second unnumbered claim in the proof of paper Theorem 5.5:", "statement": "<p>**Second unnumbered claim in the proof of paper Theorem 5.5: commuting the omitted adjunction past inherited steps.**</p><p>Under Proposition 8, fix an active \\(j\\notin S\\). Respecting the trace and named generators,\n\\[\nR_\\beta\\cong H_j(O_\\beta)\n\\qquad(j+1\\le\\beta\\le\\delta). \\tag{5.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The assertion is true initially. At an active \\(k\\in S\\) above \\(j\\), the data for both adjunctions lie in \\(O_k\\).</p><p>Assume \\(R_k=H_j(O_k)\\) under the identification already constructed. The expectation from \\(R_k\\) onto \\(O_k\\), followed by either \\(k\\)-data expectation, is a trace-preserving expectation onto that data algebra. By Proposition 2, it agrees with the expectation supplied there by the corresponding peaking sequence. The same observation applies with \\(j,k\\) interchanged. Thus the interchange assertion of Proposition 4 applies and gives\n\\[\nH_k(H_j(O_k))\\cong H_j(H_k(O_k)),\n\\]\nproving the next instance of (5.3). Other successor steps are identities. Both orders fix \\(O_k,v_j,v_k\\).</p><p>At a limit, the isomorphisms already constructed agree on overlaps because they fix the named generators. The expectations from the \\(O_\\beta\\) onto the two fixed abelian algebras generated by \\((p_n)\\) and \\((q_n^j)\\) also agree on overlaps, by Proposition 2. The limit assertion of Proposition 4 identifies the closure of the preceding \\(H_j(O_\\beta)\\) with \\(H_j\\) of the limit base, proving (5.3) there.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Countable determination and the pure-state bound:", "statement": "<p>**Countable determination and the pure-state bound: paper Lemma 5.6 and Corollary 5.7.**</p><p>Let\n\\[\nA_0=\\bigotimes_{m=1}^{\\infty}M_2,\\qquad\np_0=1,\\qquad p_n=e_{11}^{\\otimes n}\\otimes1.\n\\]\nLet \\((X_\\beta)_{\\beta\\le\\delta}\\) be a continuous bridge iteration starting from \\(A_0\\), with active steps adjoining \\(v_j\\) to join the \\(p_n\\)-state to an inequivalent state peaked by decreasing projections \\((q_n^j)\\subset X_j\\), where\n\\[\nq_0^j=1,\\qquad \\tau(q_n^j)=2^{-n}.\n\\]\nOther successor steps are identities.</p><p>For \\(T\\subset\\delta\\), put\n\\[\nA[T]=C^*(A_0,\\{v_j:j\\in T,\\ j\\text{ active}\\}).\n\\]\nChoose countable \\(I_j\\subset j\\) with \\(q_n^j\\in A[I_j]\\) for every \\(n\\), taking \\(I_j=\\varnothing\\) at inactive indices. Dependency-closed means \\(j\\in T\\Rightarrow I_j\\subset T\\).</p><p>Every pure state \\(h\\) of \\(X_\\delta\\) has a countable dependency-closed \\(S\\subset\\delta\\) such that \\(h|_{A[S]}\\) is pure and \\(h\\) is its unique state extension to \\(X_\\delta\\).</p><p>Consequently, if\n\\[\n\\mathfrak c=2^{\\aleph_0},\\qquad |\\delta|\\le\\mathfrak c,\n\\]\nthen \\(X_\\delta\\) has at most \\(\\mathfrak c\\) pure states.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 9.1 proves countable determination by a dependency-closed separable algebra. Lemma 9.2 gives the resulting cardinal bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the bridge-iteration assumptions of Proposition 9, for every pure state \\(h\\) of \\(X_\\delta\\) there is a countable dependency-closed \\(S\\subset\\delta\\) such that \\(h|_{A[S]}\\) is pure and \\(h\\) is its unique state …", "statement": "<p>Under the bridge-iteration assumptions of Proposition 9, for every pure state \\(h\\) of \\(X_\\delta\\) there is a countable dependency-closed \\(S\\subset\\delta\\) such that \\(h|_{A[S]}\\) is pure and \\(h\\) is its unique state extension to \\(X_\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Construct increasing countable dependency-closed sets \\(S_n\\), starting with \\(S_0=\\varnothing\\). Choose a countable dense subset \\(D_n\\) of \\(A[S_n]\\). For each \\(a\\in D_n\\) and each integer \\(m\\ge1\\), decreasing excision from Proposition 1 supplies a positive contraction \\(x_{a,m}\\in X_\\delta\\) satisfying\n\\[\nh(x_{a,m})=1,\\qquad\n\\|x_{a,m}(a-h(a)1)x_{a,m}\\|&lt;1/m.\n\\]\nEach witness has countable support by Proposition 7. Adjoin all these supports to \\(S_n\\) and close under dependencies to obtain \\(S_{n+1}\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nS=\\bigcup_nS_n,\\qquad B=A[S].\n\\]\nSuppose\n\\[\nh|_B=th_1+(1-t)h_2\n\\]\nfor states \\(h_1,h_2\\) and \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\). Each \\(h_i\\) takes value one on every witness \\(x_{a,m}\\). A positive contraction of state value one fixes the state’s GNS vector, so\n\\[\nh_i(a)=h_i(x_{a,m}ax_{a,m}),\\qquad h_i(x_{a,m}^2)=1.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n|h_i(a)-h(a)|\n=\n|h_i(x_{a,m}(a-h(a)1)x_{a,m})|\n&lt;1/m.\n\\]\nLetting \\(m\\) tend to infinity shows that both states agree with \\(h\\) on every \\(D_n\\). Their union is dense in \\(B\\), so \\(h|_B\\) is pure. Its unique extension is \\(h\\) by Proposition 8.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Corollary 5.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Proposition 9, put \\(\\mathfrak c=2^{\\aleph_0}\\).", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 9, put \\(\\mathfrak c=2^{\\aleph_0}\\). If \\(|\\delta|\\le\\mathfrak c\\), then \\(X_\\delta\\) has at most \\(\\mathfrak c\\) pure states.</p>", "proof": "<p>There are at most\n\\[\n\\max(\\aleph_0,|\\delta|)^{\\aleph_0}\n\\le\\mathfrak c^{\\aleph_0}\n=\\mathfrak c\n\\]\ncountable subsets of \\(\\delta\\). For each one, the corresponding algebra \\(A[S]\\) is separable and has at most \\(\\mathfrak c\\) states: values on a countable dense subset determine a state. By Lemma 9.1, every pure state of \\(X_\\delta\\) is the unique extension of one such restriction. There are at most\n\\[\n\\mathfrak c\\cdot\\mathfrak c=\\mathfrak c\n\\]\npossibilities.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 11, "cite"], [1, 19, "cite"], [1, 20, "cite"], [1, 22, "cite"], [1, 24, "cite"], [1, 27, "cite"], [1, 30, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [8, 1, "cite"], [9, 15, "cite"], [9, 19, "cite"], [9, 21, "cite"], [9, 24, "cite"], [9, 28, "cite"], [10, 1, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [12, 1, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 24, "cite"], [15, 28, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 24, "cite"], [19, 26, "cite"], [19, 27, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 24, "cite"], [21, 25, "cite"], [21, 26, "cite"], [21, 27, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"], [24, 27, "cite"], [24, 30, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"], [26, 30, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [28, 26, "cite"], [29, 0, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [30, 31, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"]]});
